<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ZAP context for novatalks.ui, referenced by dast/scan.sh's full) arm as
  -n novatalks-ui.context -U nova-ci-dast (see .github/actions/dast/scan.sh).

  NOT WIRED UP: .github/actions/dast/targets.sh's novatalks.ui/browser arm leaves
  DT_ZAP_CONTEXT empty on purpose — read that arm's comment before changing this file
  or flipping DT_ZAP_CONTEXT on. Summary of why:

  1. The login request below (URL, field names, JSON body) IS verified — both against
     this repository's own source and against the published image:
       - src/router/login/Login.vue's login() method builds
         { username, password, sso_auth_token, station } (NOT the "userLogin" v-model
         name the input field uses internally) and dispatches it to
       - src/api/auth.js's AuthAPI.login(), which POSTs that object as JSON to
         /auth/sign_in via axios (axios serialises a plain object body as JSON by
         default; there is no manual Content-Type override anywhere in the client).

  2. The <loggedinregex>/<loggedoutregex> verification below is INTENTIONALLY EMPTY.
     No reliable indicator exists, for a reason specific to this repository's
     deployment shape, not a research gap:
       - novatalks.ui ships no backend and no API base URL of any kind — grepped
         src/configurationParams.js, src/plugins/axios.client.js and
         docker/entrypoint.sh's injected VITE_APP_* var list: none names an API host.
         In production the SPA and its backend (novatalks.core) share an origin
         through an ingress this container knows nothing about; the DAST ephemeral
         scan boots novatalks.ui alone (targets.sh sets no DT_NEEDS_DB for this arm),
         so there is no server on the other end of /auth/sign_in at all.
       - Confirmed live, not just by reading code: booted
         ghcr.io/novaitdevteam/novatalks.ui:latest standalone and curled it
         (2026-09-04). POST /auth/sign_in returned "405 Not Allowed" from nginx
         itself. GET / , GET /auth/validate_token and GET /some/random/spa/route all
         returned byte-identical bodies (same ETag) — nginx's
         `try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html` serves the one static index.html shell for
         every path, logged in or not, because Vue Router renders client-side and
         nginx never looks at the response. There is no raw HTTP response ANYWHERE on
         this container — not the login response, not a poll URL, not any other
         route — that ever differs by auth state, because no server-side auth check
         exists to produce one.
       - Per the plan brief: "if you cannot find a reliable indicator for either, say
         so ... and stop rather than guessing one: a wrong indicator is worse than no
         context." A regex here would either match every response (useless) or never
         match (indistinguishable from a correct "still anonymous" reading, but for
         the wrong reason, and silently wrong the day a backend proxy is finally added
         in front of this container).

  3. Credentials: dast-api/scan.sh's `login` auth-mode generates a password with
     `openssl rand` and seeds it into the container it is about to scan via
     DEFAULT_USER_PASSWORD, because that image (novatalks.core) self-seeds an admin
     user from that env var at boot. novatalks.ui has no server-side counterpart to
     seed — it is a static nginx image with no user store — so that mechanism has
     nothing to attach to here. No credential, generated or otherwise, would ever
     authenticate against this ephemeral target; <users> is deliberately left with a
     non-functional value rather than a placeholder that could be mistaken for a real
     account.

  Revisit this file once a task actually boots novatalks.ui next to a real backend
  (e.g. novatalks.core) for the DAST scan, or proxies it through something that does.
  At that point both the verification regexes and the credential need a real answer,
  not the placeholders below.

  4. UNVERIFIED AGAINST REAL ZAP: everything above is about the login-request fields
     (URL, JSON field names) inside <authentication><config>, which genuinely are
     verified against novatalks.ui's source and its live image. The surrounding XML
     *structure* is not — there is no ZAP instance in this environment to export a real
     context from or load this one into, so the <configuration>/<context> shape below
     is written from documented ZAP conventions, not confirmed against ZAP itself.
     Specific doubts, so the next person checks these first rather than starting from
     scratch: a real ZAP context export is expected to also carry <tech>, <urlparser>,
     <postparser>, <forceduser>, <session> and <authorization> elements, none of which
     appear below; and the <users><user> line here is just "name;", where ZAP's own
     format is documented as "id;enabled;name;credentials-json". Validate this file
     against a real ZAP-exported context before setting DT_ZAP_CONTEXT for ANY
     repository, novatalks.ui or otherwise — not just before trusting the parts
     specific to novatalks.ui.
-->
<configuration>
  <context>
    <name>novatalks-ui</name>
    <desc>Login shape for novatalks.ui's /auth/sign_in. Verification and credentials are unresolved — see the header comment. Not referenced by targets.sh.</desc>
    <inscope>true</inscope>
    <incregexes>.*</incregexes>
    <excregexes></excregexes>
    <authentication>
      <!-- JSON-based Authentication Method: the request body is JSON, not the
           form-urlencoded body ZAP's classic form-based method sends. -->
      <type>4</type>
      <config>{"loginRequestUrl":"http://127.0.0.1:8000/auth/sign_in","loginRequestBody":"{\"username\":\"{%username%}\",\"password\":\"{%password%}\",\"station\":\"no_phone\"}"}</config>
      <verification>
        <method>RESPONSE</method>
        <!-- Deliberately empty — see the header comment, point 2. Do not fill these
             in with a guess; get a real backend behind this container first. -->
        <loggedinregex></loggedinregex>
        <loggedoutregex></loggedoutregex>
      </verification>
    </authentication>
    <users>
      <!-- name=nova-ci-dast, matching the -U flag scan.sh always passes when a
           context is loaded. No password: see the header comment, point 3 — nothing
           on the other end of this container could ever check one. -->
      <user>nova-ci-dast;</user>
    </users>
  </context>
</configuration>
